<!--
  Detection : LSASS Credential Dumping via PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS
  Lab       : soc-home-lab (Wazuh 4.9.2, Win10 Target + Sysmon 15.22)
  Install   : /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml
              then: systemctl restart wazuh-manager
  Rule ID   : 100004 (custom range 100000+)

  Description:
    100004  Fires on a Sysmon ProcessAccess event (Event ID 10, matched
            via sysmon_event_10 rule group) where the target process is
            lsass.exe and the requested access mask is 0x1FFFFF
            (PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS) — the access level commonly requested by
            credential-dumping tools such as Mimikatz. A negated match on
            common legitimate callers (MsMpEng.exe, wmiprvse.exe,
            svchost.exe) is included to suppress known false positives
            from this lab's own testing. Level 12.

  MITRE ATT&CK:
    T1003.001  OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory

  Prerequisites on the target:
    - Sysmon's ProcessAccess include filter must explicitly list lsass.exe
      as a TargetImage — the stock SwiftOnSecurity config does not log
      LSASS access by default.
    - LSA Protection (RunAsPPL) must be disabled
      (HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa\RunAsPPL = 0, reboot
      required) for a PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS handle to actually be grantable
      in this lab. This is a deliberately weakened lab configuration, not
      a recommended production setting — see Recommendations in the
      investigation for the opposite (hardening) guidance.

  Why not rely on the built-in rule 92900:
    Wazuh's built-in rule 92900 matches only a fixed set of access masks
    (e.g. 0x1010, 0x40) and did not fire on this test's 0x1FFFFF access.
    Querying rule.id:92900 during testing also surfaced false positives
    from legitimate system processes (svchost.exe requesting 0x101000
    against lsass.exe), confirming that the built-in rule's coverage
    neither matched this technique nor was noise-free on its own. Rule
    100004 targets the specific PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS mask instead and
    excludes the callers observed to be noisy in this environment.

  Validated against a real run:
    - A PowerShell script (run as Administrator, directly on the target)
      used OpenProcess via P/Invoke to request 0x1FFFFF access to the
      lsass.exe process ID, simulating the access pattern used by
      credential-dumping tools such as Mimikatz. No actual credential
      material was extracted — the script only obtained and printed a
      process handle.
    - Sysmon logged the access as Event ID 10 with
      SourceImage=powershell.exe, TargetImage=lsass.exe,
      GrantedAccess=0x1fffff.
    - Rule 100004 fired once (firedtimes: 1) on the verification run
      after the rule was deployed.

  Investigation: ../investigations/lsass-credential-dumping.md

  Future work:

    - Extend the negated sourceImage list as additional legitimate
      callers are identified in this environment, to keep false
      positives down without widening the access-mask match.
-->
<group name="windows,sysmon,credential_access,">

  <rule id="100004" level="12">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_10</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetImage" type="pcre2">(?i)lsass\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.grantedAccess" type="pcre2">(?i)0x1[fF]{5}</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" type="pcre2" negate="yes">(?i)(MsMpEng\.exe|wmiprvse\.exe|svchost\.exe)</field>
    <description>Custom: LSASS Credential Dumping - PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS (MITRE T1003.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
